WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.000
So there are many things to criticize from
yesterday's Robert Mueller testimony before

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.189
two different houses committees.

00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:13.059
But if we zoom out and we're honest with ourselves,
the big picture is that Robert Mueller's testimony

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:17.640
confirmed that Donald Trump is impeachable
based on what Donald Trump has done as per

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.491
the Mueller report, if Democrats want to pursue
it.

00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:24.650
And that's a question that I brought up yesterday
with you, which Democrats need to very, very

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.250
soon decide.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:30.970
Yesterday I made many of the criticisms that
could be made of the Mueller testimony.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.129
Mueller was very restrained at times.

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:34.129
He seemed confused.

00:00:34.129 --> 00:00:40.030
He often wasn't able to hear the questions
misstated even whether his own report took

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.980
a position on some things he was asked about
and then was reminded after saying he has

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.589
not taken a position on that.

00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:49.159
Well, you took a position on it in the report.

00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:54.140
At one point he said, can you remind me who
Corey Lewandowski was when he was asked about

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.140
Lewandowski?

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.589
And if you were expecting fireworks of the
explosive kind, then you certainly didn't

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:00.589
get it.

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:06.190
But Robert Mueller did quietly confirm truly
reprehensible behavior by Donald Trump, which

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:11.410
the right has been adamant about refusing
to admit even took place.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:16.410
And the major revelations during the hearing
were mostly just the moments where Robert

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:23.340
Mueller in very short answers, often with
yes or that's true, uh, confirmed the dishonesty

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.840
and the obstructive nature of Donald Trump's
behavior and actions.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.570
There isn't even any anything to play for
you.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.350
From the Republican questioning of Mueller,
their goal was to discredit Robert Mueller.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.000
They treated him like garbage.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.960
Remember Muller didn't choose to start this
probe.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.880
He was appointed to run it.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.140
He was brought in to do it.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.490
The idea that he was part of the problem or
part of some conspiracy to take down Trump

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.490
is laughable.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:53.460
And Republicans wouldn't be trying to assail
Mueller's character and the process of the

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.170
investigation itself if they didn't believe
that the content of the report was damaging

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.170
to Trump.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.170
And it is.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.521
So let's get to some of the moments where
under questioning from Democrats in the House

00:02:04.521 --> 00:02:10.379
of Representatives on these two committees,
Robert Mueller confirmed that yes, there is

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.810
impeachable action by Donald Trump.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.120
Here he was questioned by congresswoman val
Demings.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.310
Molders said that Trump's written answers
to his questions.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.819
Number one, we're under oath, and number two,
we're not answered truthfully.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:26.189
This is obstructive.

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:27.980
This is lying under oath.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:34.620
Mueller told the congresswoman that Trump's
answers were quote, generally untruthful.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.049
This is a massive indictment on Donald Trump's
behavior.

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:40.980
He lied under oath to a federal investigators.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.469
Mueller exposed that due to limited scope
of, of the investigation.

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:51.329
There are huge areas about which we still
know very, very little and investigations

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:52.709
are actually continuing.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:54.189
That is something Republicans have said.

00:02:54.189 --> 00:02:57.310
The opposite of Republicans have said the
scope was too wide.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.659
Mueller confirmed yesterday the scope, if
anything, was actually too narrow.

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:08.480
Molar confirmed that White House counsel,
Don McGahn, was pressured to lie by the White

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:15.560
House about whether he was ever asked by Donald
Trump to fire the former special counsel,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.659
Robert Mueller.

00:03:16.659 --> 00:03:19.629
This is an impeachable offense in and of itself.

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:25.459
If Democrats wanted to take it up, quote from
Democrat Karen Bass, the president told the

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:31.500
White House Staff Secretary Rob Porter to
try to pressure Don McGahn to make a false

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:32.620
denial.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.029
Is that correct?

00:03:34.029 --> 00:03:37.650
To which Robert Mueller said, that's correct.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.760
That's a big deal.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.909
It's easy to get lost in the scandal numbness
and in Robert Mueller, you know, seeming a

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:48.629
little shaky up there, but this is huge that
this is obstruction of justice period.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:53.909
Mueller confirmed that a president can be
indicted after leaving office

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:56.519
DOJ Under Department of Justice Policy.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:01.689
The president could be prosecuted for obstruction
of justice crimes after he leaves offices.

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:02.689
Correct.

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:03.689
Thank you.

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:09.439
Now we knew that molar implied it in his report
and in the hearing, Mueller made it abundantly

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:14.599
clear that that's the case and this came up
against a, this came up again rather with

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:20.510
Congressman Ken Buck, which is interesting
because had Ken buck known that Robert Mueller

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.900
was going to answer in this way, my guess
is he wouldn't even have gone down this path.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:26.310
Take a look.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:31.120
The, uh, could you charge the president with
a trial after he left office?

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.120
Yes.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.379
You believe that he committed, you could charge
the president nine states with obstruction

00:04:35.379 --> 00:04:37.520
of justice after he left office?

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.789
Yes, ethically under the ethical standards.

00:04:39.789 --> 00:04:44.150
Well, I, I, I'm not certain because I haven't
looked at the ethical standards, but the LLC

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:49.389
[inaudible] opinion says that the prosecutor
y can not bring a charge against the sitting

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:50.389
